0.020782470703125, -0.0003230571746826172, 0.018524169921875, -0.0013303756713867188, 0.0015573501586914062, 0.035675048828125, -0.0006818771362304688, -0.0119781494140625, 0.0179595947265625, 0.024078369140625, -0.0139617919921875, 0.005283355712890625, -0.02923583984375, -0.01445770263671875, -0.0166168212890625, 0.00482177734375, 0.0272979736328125, -0.005889892578125, -0.0033779144287109375, -0.0027484893798828125, -0.017242431640625, 0.0008382797241210938, 0.0015344619750976562, 0.0197296142578125, 0.04669189453125, -0.0290069580078125, -0.01003265380859375, -0.00567626953125, 0.0031452178955078125, -0.02227783203125, 0.01552581787109375, 0.0102081298828125, 0.010162353515625, 0.01352691650390625, -0.03497314453125, -0.015655517578125, -0.0225067138671875, -0.006481170654296875, 0.03369140625, -0.018218994140625, 0.0188446044921875, -0.056304931640625, 0.0084228515625, -0.0205230712890625, -0.0175323486328125, -0.002674102783203125, 0.005855560302734375, -0.02093505859375, -0.0081329345703125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "This short work presents a novel extension of the permanental process model by incorporating fixed effects, which, to our knowledge, have not been addressed in prior work. Existing permanental process models generally lack explicit fixed effects, limiting their ability to directly model known covariates such as experimental conditions or baseline trends. This omission restricts interpretability and may reduce estimation accuracy in scientific and applied settings where covariate information is available.\n\nTo address this gap, we introduce fixed effects into the permanental process by extending the model to include a linear component for known covariates. Specifically, we place a Gaussian prior with variance $\\tau^2$ on the coefficients of the fixed effects. Considering the \"diffuse prior limit,\" we let $\\tau^2 \\to \\infty$, so the prior becomes non-informative and the fixed effect coefficients are estimated solely from the data. Formally, if $X$ denotes the design matrix of covariates and $\\beta$ the vector of coefficients, the diffuse prior corresponds to $p(\\beta) \\propto \\lim_{\\tau^2 \\to \\infty} \\mathcal{N}(0, \\tau^2 I)$, resulting in no penalization on the fixed effects. In practice, this means fixed effects are unconstrained and can be interpreted directly, but the approach assumes that the covariates are not collinear and are observed without error.\n\nApplying the representer theorem in this setting, we show that the solution to the regularized estimation problem for the intensity function $\\lambda$ of the permanental process decomposes as $\\lambda(x) = x^\\top \\hat{\\beta} + f(x)$, where $f$ lies in a Reproducing Kernel Hilbert Space (RKHS) associated with a kernel $k$. The coefficients $\\hat{\\beta}$ are determined by the data, and $f$ is a weighted combination of kernel functions evaluated at the observed data points. The estimation problem thus involves minimizing a regularized loss, where regularization is applied only to $f$ in the RKHS. A schematic example would be modeling neural spike counts as a function of stimulus orientation (a fixed effect) plus flexible residual structure. This two-part decomposition clarifies how known experimental variables and unknown structure are handled separately in the model.\n\nThe regularization penalty on $f$ induces an equivalent kernel that depends on the inclusion of fixed effects. In the \"limiting equivalent kernel\" regime\u2014referenced for example in Wahba (1990), Section 1.4\u2014the kernel is modified by projecting out the component spanned by the fixed effects, leading to a kernel $k^*(x, x')$ that satisfies $\\sum_j X_{ij} k^*(x_j, x') = 0$ for all $i$. This construction ensures the RKHS norm does not penalize the fixed effects, unlike standard kernels. The novelty here lies in applying this to permanental processes, where the interaction between the permanent-based likelihood and the RKHS structure introduces unique statistical properties.\n\nThe RKHS structure and the associated regularization penalty guide model selection by favoring smoothness or structure in $f$ compatible with the chosen kernel, while leaving fixed effect estimates unbiased by the penalty. This dual structure allows explicit modeling of variables such as treatment groups, time trends, or sensor location (as fixed effects), with the RKHS component capturing unknown or complex variations. For example, in neural encoding, stimulus attributes may enter as fixed effects, while the RKHS handles nonparametric dependencies. Such modeling improves interpretability because domain knowledge can be separated from data-driven flexibility.\n\nRegarding computation, estimation can proceed via penalized likelihood or empirical Bayes, similar to other kernel methods, but may face challenges such as evaluating permanents (typically intractable) and scalability with large data. Methods for scalable approximate inference and variable selection with fixed effects are promising directions for future development and will be addressed in subsequent work."}}